insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qi-study-asks', 1, 'Which of these does the course leave to the interpreter, with no engine function doing it?', '["Returning the exact plane-wave reflection coefficient at each angle for the zone top, which zoeppritzRpp computes.", "Fitting measured Vs on Vp by least squares and giving the prediction interval, which fitVsRegression and predictVs return.", "Deciding whether the QI study should go ahead; a person writes that verdict from the spectra, trends, moduli and reflection coefficients.", "Averaging the power of many traces into one spectrum and taking the root, which averageSpectrum does on the stated traces."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engines return spectra, trends, moduli and reflection coefficients, and the verdict is written by the interpreter from those numbers. zoeppritzRpp, fitVsRegression with predictVs, and averageSpectrum are all engine functions the course calls, the last one on the 120 spread traces.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qi-study-asks', 2, 'The Ekene fluids are computed at 180 degF. Where does that temperature come from when an engine runs?', '["From the caller, as a stated input; the engines hold no Ekene figure.", "From a table of Ekene conditions held inside fluids.js, which brine falls back on whenever the caller passes no temperature.", "From the well header file and the clock, which the engine reads at run time so the conditions match the date of the logs.", "From a random draw around 180 degF made on each call."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every temperature, pressure, salinity and oil gravity is an input the caller states, and the course states 180 degF, 3200 psia, salinity 0.035000 and 32 API with the kit. No engine reads a file, a clock or a random number of its own, and none keeps an Ekene table.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qi-study-asks', 3, 'What is the Ekene field that the teaching pack is cut from?', '["A producing field in the Niger Delta whose well, company and survey names were changed before the pack was cut.", "A published benchmark field from Batzle and Wang (1992), reprinted with its logs.", "Figures measured in the QI with Petrolord videos, cut to four wells.", "A synthetic demonstration field: one earth model, with every log and trace generated by a stated script."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Ekene field is the Petrolord demonstration field, generated from one earth model by a stated script so every answer can be checked against the model. No real well, company or survey appears. Batzle and Wang (1992) is the reference for brine and live oil properties, and the pack is cut from release ekene-demo-v3 by the course''s cut script.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qi-study-asks', 4, 'At Ekene-2 the top of the Ekene Sand is at 5134.514436 ft and the oil-water contact at 5118.110236 ft. What does the oil leg selection return there?', '["65 samples, the same as Ekene-1, because the contact sits at one depth in every well and so cuts every sand alike.", "No samples: the oil leg is 0 and the water leg 187.", "114 samples, since above the contact the selection keeps going up into the Ogbia Shale until it finds net sand.", "130 samples, the cap rock count, which the selection reads as the oil leg when the whole sand lies below the contact."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A well whose top of the Ekene Sand lies below the oil-water contact has no oil leg by the rule, and the course tables Ekene-2 at net sand 187, oil leg 0, water leg 187. 65 is Ekene-1''s oil leg, 114 is Ekene-3''s, and 130 is the cap rock at every pack well.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qi-study-asks', 5, 'In the course''s vocabulary, a distance from the wet trend is signed. Which side is negative?', '["The brine side, so a wet sand reads negative and a hydrocarbon sand positive.", "The hydrocarbon side.", "Neither: a perpendicular distance is a magnitude, so the course prints it without a sign.", "The hydrocarbon side when read from the published line, and the brine side from the fitted line."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The distance is the signed perpendicular distance of an (A, B) point from a stated line, and negative is the hydrocarbon side whichever line is stated. At Ekene-1 the top reads -0.072091 in situ against -0.004519 with brine, so the oil moves it further to the negative side.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qi-study-asks', 6, 'A learner types an interval level of 1.2 into predictVs in the feasibility explorer. What does the panel show?', '["A prediction at the nearest valid level, 0.900000, with a warning line saying the stated level was replaced.", "A prediction with NaN in its low and high fields.", "The engine''s own words: \"The sample interval must be positive.\", since the level is read as an interval.", "The engine''s own words: \"The interval level must be between 0 and 1.\""]'::jsonb, 3, 'When an engine cannot answer it throws, and the panel prints its message unchanged; predictVs at a level of 1.2 gives "The interval level must be between 0 and 1." Nothing is replaced, NaN fields belong to elasticPoint, which does not throw, and the sample interval message is amplitudeSpectrum''s refusal of a 0 ms interval.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qi-study-asks', 7, 'Which samples does the course''s cap rock selection take at Ekene-1?', '["The 65 ft of log above the top of the Ekene Sand, 130 samples.", "The whole Ogbia Shale, from its top down to the sand.", "The oil leg, 65 samples of net sand below the top.", "Every sample inside the Ekene Sand with Vsh above 0.350000, the shale the net sand cut-off leaves out."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The cap rock is the 65 ft of log above the top of the Ekene Sand, 130 samples at every pack well, read as one layer by its means. The net sand above the contact is the oil leg, 65 samples at Ekene-1, and the Ekene-1 logs start at 4931.500000 ft, far below the top of the Ogbia Shale at 4232.283465 ft.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qi-study-asks', 8, 'How does the course treat the two gassmann.js messages that are missing from its table of refusals?', '["It quotes both in full beside the other 21 refusals, because every engine message is printed between quotation marks.", "It leaves them out because substituteVels never throws, and reports a bad porosity as a result with NaN fields.", "It names each by what it refuses and does not quote it, because each carries a punctuation mark the course does not print.", "It counts them among the 21 refusals across 14 functions, and prints them under elasticPoint, which covers the same inputs."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Two more messages exist in gassmann.js, an implied saturated modulus that is not positive and an inverse Gassmann dry modulus that is not positive; the course names them and does not print them. substituteVels does throw, for example "Porosity must be in (0, 1)." at a porosity of 0, and the 21 refusals are the tabled ones.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qi-study-asks', 9, 'The Ekene-1 oil-leg AI (acoustic impedance) of the means is 6639018.603084 kg/(m2 s). A second source writes the same rock as 6639.018603. Which unit is that source using?', '["Units of 10^6 kg/(m2 s), the form a crossplot axis uses to keep impedance in single figures.", "SI, the shear impedance Vs x density.", "kg/(m2 s) with the density taken from the oil leg''s pore fluid in place of the bulk log density.", "(m/s)(g/cc), that is AI divided by 1000."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The same AI is 6639018.603084 in kg/(m2 s), 6.639019 in units of 10^6 kg/(m2 s) and 6639.018603 in (m/s)(g/cc). The oil-leg SI is 3795337.170439 kg/(m2 s), and AI is Vp times the bulk density of the leg, 2316.676923 kg/m3.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qi-study-asks', 10, 'At Ekene-1 the top of the Ekene Sand reads OWT 644.398189 ms, linear between two checkshots. What time should a learner look for on the seismic?', '["644.398189 ms, since the seismic and the checkshot both record one-way time from the reference.", "TWT 1288.796378 ms.", "585.055000 ms, the one-way time of the checkshot at 4687.500000 ft above the top.", "644.438000 ms, the time of the checkshot at 5079.002625 ft, the level nearest the top."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Seismic time is two-way time and a checkshot records one-way time, so the top at OWT 644.398189 ms is at TWT 1288.796378 ms. 585.055000 and 644.438000 ms are the one-way times of the bracketing checkshots, and both are one-way.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qi-study-asks', 11, 'On the kit''s SEG normal polarity seismic, how does the top of the Ekene Sand appear at Ekene-1?', '["As a peak, a positive number, because AI (acoustic impedance) increases downwards from the Ogbia Shale into the harder sand.", "As a trough, since SEG normal polarity writes an increase in impedance downwards as a negative number.", "As a trough, because oil softens the sand at Ekene-1 below the AI (acoustic impedance) of the shale above it.", "As a peak only where the sand is wet; at Ekene-1 the oil leg''s lower AI (acoustic impedance) makes it a trough."]'::jsonb, 0, 'On SEG normal polarity an increase in acoustic impedance downwards is a peak, a positive number. At Ekene-1 the cap rock''s mean AI is 4697628.723902 kg/(m2 s) and the oil leg''s 6639018.603084, so even with oil the sand is harder and its top is a peak.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qi-study-asks', 12, 'The kit writes the top of the Ekene Sand at Ekene-1 as 1548.000000 m. What does the course print for it?', '["1548.000000 ft, the kit''s depth kept as written.", "5118.110236 ft, the kit''s 1560.000000 m converted.", "5078.740157 ft, converted from the kit''s metres by feet = metres / 0.3048.", "4232.283465 ft, the top of the Ogbia Shale."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Depths are feet throughout, feet = metres / 0.3048, and the top at Ekene-1 is 5078.740157 ft (1548.000000 m in the kit). 5118.110236 ft is the oil-water contact (1560.000000 m) and 4232.283465 ft is the top of the Ogbia Shale.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qi-study-asks', 13, 'Which of these could be a graded capstone figure at the Associate tier?', '["An engine''s return value on a held-out synthetic case that the learner pastes into the feasibility explorer.", "The Ekene median signal to noise of 361.155122, read from the 800 to 1600 ms window as the verdict quotes it.", "The usability grade of Ekene-9, missing, since a grade is a return value of QI Studio''s own function on the headers.", "A figure spoken in a QI with Petrolord video, since the videos run the same engines on the Ekene field."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every graded field is an engine return value on a held-out synthetic case supplied as a case file, and the case is never Ekene. No Ekene figure, no usability grade and no figure in the videos is graded; the videos speak the Ekene answers aloud, which is why Ekene is never graded.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qi-study-asks', 14, 'Which reference does the course name for signal to noise from trace coherency?', '["Castagna, Swan and Foster (1998), whose background trend the course also uses to read noise off a crossplot.", "Goodway, Chen and Downton (1997), the source of the elastic set''s lambda-rho and mu-rho.", "Rutherford and Williams (1989), with class IV, whose classes sort a trace by its signal.", "Hatton, Worthington and Makin (1986)."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Signal to noise from coherency is Hatton, Worthington and Makin (1986), in seismicQc.js. Castagna, Swan and Foster (1998) is the wet background trend, Goodway, Chen and Downton (1997) is lambda-rho and mu-rho, and Rutherford and Williams (1989) is the AVO classes.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qi-study-asks', 15, 'How does the course use the published references behind its engines?', '["It quotes each paper''s own explanation with credit, since a method is best taught in its authors'' words.", "It names each by its authors and year and teaches it by concept and equation in its own words, quoting none of their prose.", "It prints the engines'' source comments as its teaching text, since they restate each paper exactly.", "It re-derives every method in full, Batzle-Wang, Gassmann and Shuey among them, in each lesson that uses one."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every method is named with its authors and year and the course quotes none of their prose, teaching each by concept and equation. The engines'' source comments are provenance, and the Rock Physics course teaches Batzle-Wang, Gassmann and Shuey in full while this course recaps each in one lesson.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-data-inventory', 1, 'Which two curves does the course say rock physics needs from a well before it can run at all?', '["A shear sonic and a gamma ray log.", "A sonic for Vp, and a density log.", "Porosity and saturation curves.", "Checkshots and a deviation survey, because without them the well cannot be placed in time or depth."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Rock physics needs Vp, from a sonic, and density; sonic and density are the two required items, graded missing when absent. Shear, porosity and saturation are limited when absent, checkshots tie the well to the seismic, and the survey places it in depth, with fewer than two checkshot levels and no survey both limited.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-data-inventory', 2, 'What does a measured shear log change about a well''s results in a QI study?', '["It lets rock physics run at all, so a well without shear is graded missing.", "Nothing, since QI computes Vs from Vp by Greenberg and Castagna at every well.", "It ties the well to the seismic in time.", "The shear-dependent results rest on a measurement; without one they rest on an estimate."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A measured shear log means the shear-dependent results rest on a measurement, where without one they rest on an estimate; shear is limited when absent because the study can go on with an estimate. Rock physics runs on Vp and density, so a well without shear is limited, and checkshots are what tie the well to the seismic; every logged Ekene well carries DTS.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-data-inventory', 3, 'Which logged Ekene well has no density log, and what does the matrix grade its cell?', '["Ekene-9, graded missing; its density item reads \"No density over the zone.\"", "Ekene-9, graded limited, since density can be estimated as shear is.", "Ekene-10, the one well with 12 curves, graded limited.", "Ekene-11, the planned well, graded missing on every item."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ekene-9''s curves are CALI, GR, DT, DTS and RT, so it is the well without density, and density is a required item graded missing when absent. Ekene-10 carries density, and Ekene-11 has no logs, so it is not among the 10 graded cells.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-data-inventory', 4, 'Ekene-1 grades good on sonic, density, shear, checkshots, datum and survey, and limited on porosity, shale volume and saturation. What is its cell?', '["Good: six of its nine items are good.", "Missing: three limited items add up to one missing item.", "Limited: a cell takes the worst of its items.", "Good for the elastic work and limited for the substitution: two cells."]'::jsonb, 2, 'QI Studio grades each well item by item and a cell takes the worst of its items, so Ekene-1''s cell is limited. The matrix prints one cell per well and target interval, and nine of the ten Ekene cells are limited this way.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-data-inventory', 5, 'Why does no cell in the Ekene usability matrix grade good?', '["Every logged well lacks a deviation survey, which grades limited and so holds every cell below good.", "Fewer than two checkshot levels were recorded in each logged well, which grades the checkshot item limited.", "The LAS files carry no porosity, shale volume or saturation curve, so those three items read as absent and limited at every logged well.", "Shear is estimated at every well, and an estimate is graded limited whatever curves the files carry."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The matrix reads the porosity, shale volume and saturation curves the LAS files carry, and they carry none of the three, so each is limited and the counts are good 0, limited 9, missing 1. The survey, checkshot and shear items are good at every limited well, with 21 or more checkshot levels and a DTS log in each.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-data-inventory', 6, 'How many issues does QI Studio suggest on the Ekene matrix, and of what severity?', '["31: 1 high and 30 medium.", "10, one per graded well.", "3, one for each item kind below good, each raised once for the whole field.", "32: 1 high, 30 medium, and 1 more raised for Ekene-11 as a planned well with no logs."]'::jsonb, 0, 'suggestedIssues raises one issue per well and item kind that is not good: 31 issues on this matrix, 1 high and 30 medium. The high one is Ekene-9''s density, and the thirty medium ones are the three curve items at each of the 10 logged wells; Ekene-11 is not in the matrix.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-data-inventory', 7, 'A logged well has no deviation survey. What issue does QI Studio suggest for that item?', '["A high issue, since a well that cannot be placed in depth is missing.", "None: suggestedIssues lists missing items only.", "A high issue with the density log remedy.", "A medium issue, since no deviation survey grades limited."]'::jsonb, 3, 'No deviation survey is limited, and a limited item raises a medium issue while a missing one raises a high issue. The register lists limited items too, which is where its 30 medium issues come from, and the density remedy belongs to a missing density log.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-data-inventory', 8, 'What does the user do with each issue that QI Studio suggests?', '["Nothing: each line closes on its own when the data arrives.", "Keeps, edits or dismisses it.", "Accepts or rejects it all.", "Grades it for the capstone."]'::jsonb, 1, 'QI Studio suggests the issues and the user keeps, edits or dismisses each one. No usability grade or category is ever graded, and every capstone field is an engine return value on a held-out synthetic case.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-data-inventory', 9, 'Besides sonic and density, which item does the usability rule grade missing when it is absent?', '["An unset elevation.", "A shear log, since without measured Vs every result that depends on shear stops at that well.", "Fewer than two checkshot levels, since the well then cannot be tied to the seismic in time.", "No deviation survey, since the depths of a deviated well cannot be read straight down."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Sonic and density are missing when absent, and an unset elevation is missing too. Shear is limited when absent because the study can go on with an estimate, and fewer than two checkshot levels and no deviation survey are both limited.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-data-inventory', 10, 'A logged well carries one checkshot level. How does the usability rule grade its checkshot item?', '["Missing, because a checkshot is what ties a well to the seismic, and no tie can be made from a single level.", "Good, since one level is enough to tie the well and only a well with no checkshots at all is graded below good.", "Limited.", "Missing for a vertical well and limited for a deviated one, which also carries its deviation survey."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Fewer than two checkshot levels is limited under QI Studio''s rule, whatever the kind of well. On Ekene every logged well carries 21 or more levels, so the item is good everywhere.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-data-inventory', 11, 'Ekene-11 is planned and Ekene-12 is survey_only. How do they enter the Ekene usability matrix?', '["Both graded missing on every item, each a high issue.", "Both graded limited, since a planned well can be logged.", "Ekene-11 is graded missing on density and Ekene-12 limited on its deviation survey item.", "They do not: neither has logs, and the matrix holds the 10 logged wells."]'::jsonb, 3, '10 of 12 wells have logs, and the counts are over those 10 cells: good 0, limited 9, missing 1. The register''s single high issue is Ekene-9''s density, so neither Ekene-11 nor Ekene-12 raises one.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-data-inventory', 12, 'Of the 10 logged Ekene wells, how many carry a density log?', '["10, since every logged well that carries a sonic also carries a density log in the inventory.", "9.", "4, the pack wells Ekene-1 to Ekene-4, which are the only wells with RHOB in their curves.", "12, counting the planned well and the survey location, which are graded as if logged."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Of the 10 logged wells, 10 carry a shear sonic, 9 a density log and 10 two or more checkshot levels. Ekene-9 is the well without density, and Ekene-5 to Ekene-10 apart from Ekene-9 carry RHOB as well as the pack wells.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-data-inventory', 13, 'Where does the course say porosity, shale volume and saturation curves come from in practice?', '["From the LAS files of each well.", "From the Greenberg-Castagna regression, the published estimate a limited item falls back on when a curve is absent.", "From the engines, which compute porosity and saturation from Vp and density whenever the caller leaves them out.", "From Petrophysics Studio, before the QI study starts."]'::jsonb, 3, 'In practice the porosity, shale volume and saturation curves come from Petrophysics Studio before a QI study starts, which is why the pack carries the kit''s PHIT, VSH and SW for Ekene-1 to Ekene-4. The LAS files carry none of the three, Greenberg-Castagna estimates shear from Vp, and the engines take porosity and saturation as stated inputs.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-data-inventory', 14, 'Which logged Ekene wells are deviated, so that each needs its survey to be placed in depth?', '["Ekene-8, Ekene-9 and Ekene-10.", "Ekene-1 to Ekene-4, the pack wells.", "Ekene-11 and Ekene-12, the two wells with no logs.", "Ekene-5, Ekene-6 and Ekene-7."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The inventory lists Ekene-8, Ekene-9 and Ekene-10 as deviated, each with 24 checkshot levels and a survey graded good. The pack wells and Ekene-5 to Ekene-7 are vertical, Ekene-11 is planned and Ekene-12 is survey_only.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-data-inventory', 15, 'What remedy does QI Studio suggest for Ekene-9''s one high issue?', '["Estimate density from Vp by Greenberg and Castagna.", "\"Request the density log; rock physics cannot run without it.\"", "Dismiss it, since Ekene-9 has shear and 24 checkshot levels.", "Request a shear log, the curve Ekene-9 lacks."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The suggested remedy for a missing density log is "Request the density log; rock physics cannot run without it." Greenberg-Castagna estimates shear, Ekene-9''s five curves are CALI, GR, DT, DTS and RT, so it has DTS, and a remedy is suggested for the user to keep, edit or dismiss.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-seismic-qc', 1, 'What sets the frequency step of the spectrum seismicQc.js returns?', '["The taper fraction, since a heavier Tukey taper spreads each frequency over more steps.", "The number of traces averaged, the step shrinking as more traces enter the spectrum.", "The window length in ms alone, read before any zero padding is added.", "1000 / (padded length x sample interval in ms), which is 0.488281 Hz on the Ekene window sampled every 4.000000 ms."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Each trace is padded with zeros to twice the next power of two samples, and the step is 1000 / (padded length x sample interval in ms): 0.488281 Hz at 4.000000 ms on the Ekene window. The taper shapes the trace ends and the traces are averaged after the transform, so neither enters the step.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-seismic-qc', 2, 'How does averageSpectrum combine the 120 spread traces into one spectrum?', '["It averages their amplitudes at each frequency, the mean of the one-sided moduli.", "It averages the power of the traces and takes the root.", "It keeps the single trace with the highest peak amplitude, 3269.099145, and discards the rest of the traces as noise.", "It stacks the traces in time into one trace and transforms that stacked trace, so the spectrum is of the stack."]'::jsonb, 1, 'averageSpectrum averages the POWER of many traces and takes the root, giving one spectrum of the 120 traces with peak 31.374887 Hz. The peak amplitude of 3269.099145 belongs to that mean spectrum, refined by a parabola, and the engine transforms each trace before averaging.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-seismic-qc', 3, 'A learner pastes an RMS slice of 3 by 3 into the footprint function. What does the panel show?', '["A result with share 0.000000 and no stripe.", "The engine''s own words: \"A spectrum needs at least 8 samples.\"", "The engine''s own words: \"A footprint needs a slice of at least 4 by 4.\"", "A footprint by the rule, since three lines in each direction give every period searched at least one full cycle."]'::jsonb, 2, 'footprint refuses a slice of 3 by 3 with "A footprint needs a slice of at least 4 by 4.", and the panel prints the message unchanged. The 8-sample message is amplitudeSpectrum''s refusal of a 5-sample trace, and a refusal returns no share and calls no stripe.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-seismic-qc', 4, 'On a 30 Hz Ricker wavelet the engine reads a peak of 30.070572 Hz against the closed form''s 30.000000 Hz. What does the course put the difference down to?', '["The sampling and the finite length of the wavelet.", "The 0.100000 taper the engine applies to every trace before the transform.", "Averaging the 120 spread traces, which pulls the peak towards the field''s 31.374887 Hz.", "An error in the engine, which the course corrects by quoting the closed-form figures only."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The wavelet was sampled every 4.000000 ms with 31 samples and no taper, and the small differences (edges 14.463999 and 49.112032 Hz against 14.471033 and 49.062252 Hz) are the sampling and the finite length. It is one wavelet, so no traces are averaged.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-seismic-qc', 5, 'Where does spectrumStats place a -6 dB edge?', '["Where the amplitude falls to 10^(-6/20) of the peak amplitude, interpolated linearly between frequency steps, below and above the peak.", "6 Hz either side of the peak frequency, so the -6 dB band is always 12 Hz wide.", "Where the amplitude falls to 10^(-6/10) of the peak, the decibel form the course uses for power ratios.", "Where the amplitude falls 6 dB below the mean amplitude of the window."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The -6 dB edges are where the amplitude falls to 10^(-6/20) of the peak amplitude, below and above the peak; on Ekene they are 14.425434 and 34.787627 Hz, a bandwidth of 20.362193 Hz. The spectrum is an amplitude, so its decibels use 20 log10, and the edges are read against the peak.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-seismic-qc', 6, 'On the Ekene full stack, 800 to 1600 ms, the centroid is 26.656573 Hz. What is the centroid?', '["The frequency of the peak, refined between steps by a parabola through the three highest values.", "The midpoint of the two -6 dB edges, halfway across the band that carries the useful energy.", "The power-weighted mean frequency, the sum of f A(f)^2 over the sum of A(f)^2.", "The amplitude-weighted mean frequency, the sum of f A(f) over the sum of A(f)."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The centroid is the power-weighted mean frequency, the sum of f A(f)^2 over the sum of A(f)^2. The peak in the same window is 31.374887 Hz, a different figure, and the definition weights by power, the amplitude squared.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-seismic-qc', 7, 'Going from the 800 to 1600 ms window to the 1600 to 2400 ms window, what happens to the Ekene mean spectrum?', '["The peak holds at 31.374887 Hz while the -6 dB low edge falls to 7.272835 Hz as the deeper data loses its low end.", "The peak falls from 31.374887 to 21.761176 Hz, while the -6 dB low edge barely moves, from 14.425434 to 14.429678 Hz.", "The peak rises to 34.787627 Hz, since the deeper window holds more reflectors and so more high-frequency energy.", "Every figure stays the same to the decimals, because only the window changes and the traces are the same."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The peak falls with depth, 31.374887 to 21.761176 Hz, as the earth absorbs the higher frequencies, and the -6 dB edges move less: the low edge goes from 14.425434 to 14.429678 Hz. 7.272835 Hz is the deep window''s -20 dB low edge, and 34.787627 Hz is the shallow window''s -6 dB high edge.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-seismic-qc', 8, 'The same 120 traces and the 800 to 1600 ms window are measured with a taper of 0.500000 in place of 0.100000. What peak does the engine return?', '["31.374887 Hz unchanged, because the taper only shapes the trace ends, which leaves the peak of the mean spectrum where it was.", "26.175193 Hz, the figure the engine returns at that taper, which is where the peak of the mean spectrum now sits.", "31.323187 Hz, since a heavier taper only nudges the peak of a broad mean spectrum by a few hundredths of a hertz.", "22.305063 Hz, against 31.374887 Hz at 0.100000."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The taper is a stated choice: at 0.500000 the peak is 22.305063 Hz, against 31.374887 Hz at 0.100000. 26.175193 Hz is the centroid at 0.500000 and 31.323187 Hz is the peak at 0.250000, so a spectrum figure is quoted with its taper.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-seismic-qc', 9, 'The single trace at Ekene-1, same window and taper, gives -6 dB edges at 30.556053 and 32.127840 Hz. Which band belongs in the verdict?', '["The single trace''s band, 30.556053 to 32.127840 Hz, because the trace sits at the well where the sand is.", "The Ricker band, 14.471033 to 49.062252 Hz.", "The mean spectrum''s -6 dB band of the 120 spread traces, 14.425434 to 34.787627 Hz.", "The -20 dB band, 8.801471 to 47.968080 Hz."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The -6 dB band is read from the mean spectrum of stated traces, and the verdict quotes 14.425434 to 34.787627 Hz. A single trace''s spectrum is spiky from a few reflectors, so its band is a sliver around its peak of 31.418177 Hz, and the Ricker figures check the engine on a wavelet.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-seismic-qc', 10, 'The median signal to noise on the shallow window is 361.155122, quoted as 25.576938 dB. Which conversion gives the decibels?', '["10 log10 of the ratio, because signal to noise is a power ratio.", "20 log10 of the ratio, the same form the amplitude spectrum uses for its decibels.", "c / (1 - c) of the ratio, the Hatton, Worthington and Makin form for decibels.", "The ratio divided by 10."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Signal to noise is a POWER ratio from trace coherency, with its decibel form 10 log10 of the ratio: 361.155122 is 25.576938 dB. The spectrum uses 20 log10 because it is an amplitude, and c / (1 - c) turns a correlation into the ratio.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-seismic-qc', 11, 'Two neighbouring traces are almost identical, so their correlation c is close to 1. How should the engine''s signal to noise be read?', '["As the true ratio, exact at every correlation.", "As a refusal: the engine throws \"No neighbouring pair had samples to correlate.\" once c reaches 1.", "As zero, since identical traces carry no noise.", "As a ceiling: c is clamped at 0.999999, where the ratio is 999998.999971."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine clamps c at 0.999999 at most, and there the ratio is 999998.999971, so a figure that large is a ceiling set by the clamp. QI Studio prints any ratio of 10,000 or more as "over 10,000 (over 40 dB)" for that reason. The pair message is the refusal of two traces of nulls.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-seismic-qc', 12, 'On the shallow window the median signal to noise is 361.155122 at maximum lags of 0, 1, 3 and 6 samples. What does the course give as the reason?', '["The engine reads the lag only on the deep window and fixes it at zero on the shallow one.", "The best alignment of every neighbouring pair is at zero lag.", "The median ignores the lag by design, which only the mean ratio of the pairs would respond to.", "The clamp at 0.999999 is reached on every pair, so a longer search cannot raise the ratio."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The shallow window returns the same median at every lag because the best alignment of every neighbouring pair here is at zero lag. The lag is a stated choice the engine applies to every window, and the pairs read 388.889121, 471.612452 and so on, far below the clamp''s ratio of 999998.999971.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-seismic-qc', 13, 'From the 21 neighbouring traces along inline 1027, what does snrFromCoherency report for a window?', '["The median ratio over the 20 neighbouring pairs, and the same in decibels.", "The mean ratio over 21 pairs, since each trace is paired once with the trace beside it and once with itself.", "The ratio of the first pair, 388.889121, since the first pair sits at Ekene-1.", "The largest pair ratio, 471.612452, as the best the data can do."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine takes every neighbouring pair and reports the median ratio and the same in decibels; 21 traces give 20 pairs, with a median of 361.155122 (25.576938 dB) on the shallow window. 388.889121 and 471.612452 are the first two pairs, and neither is the median.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-seismic-qc', 14, 'The Ekene slice along the crosslines gives share 0.015148, prominence 27.724874 and 8.500000 cycles. By QI Studio''s rule, is it a footprint?', '["Yes: a prominence above 10 with more than five cycles is enough to call a stripe.", "No: 8.500000 cycles falls short of the rule''s minimum of 10 repeats of the period across the slice.", "No: the share must be above 0.3, and the rule needs all three conditions together.", "Yes: any periodic stripe the engine finds in the band of periods searched is a footprint."]'::jsonb, 2, 'QI Studio calls a footprint only with a share above 0.3, a prominence of 10 or more and at least five cycles. Here the prominence and cycles pass and the share of 0.015148 fails, while the designed stripe passes with share 0.490123, prominence 467.977933 and 21.500000 cycles.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-seismic-qc', 15, 'Why does the course measure a footprint on a slice of RMS amplitude?', '["Because the footprint function throws on any negative sample.", "Because RMS amplitude raises the share of every stripe above 0.3.", "Because only the RMS slice holds the Ekene Sand.", "On signed seismic a stripe of gain averages away when the slice is averaged to a profile."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine averages the slice along one axis to a profile, and on signed seismic a stripe of gain averages away, so it is measured on RMS or absolute amplitude. The footprint function''s one refusal is of a slice under 4 by 4, the Ekene RMS slice shows a share of only 0.015148, and the spread traces of 800 to 1600 ms also hold the Ekene Sand.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-elastic-logs-at-the-wells', 1, 'The feasibility explorer passes elasticPoint a sample whose Vs is above its Vp. What comes back?', '["A refusal in the engine''s own words, \"Upper halfspace has vs >= vp.\", which stops the whole layer.", "A refusal in the engine''s own words, \"vp, vs and rho must be positive.\", as Gassmann''s check on its inputs.", "A result with every field NaN, AI and Vp/Vs included, and the panel shows the gap.", "A result with Vs clamped to the sample''s Vp, so AI is finite and Vp/Vs reads exactly one."]'::jsonb, 2, 'elasticPoint with Vs above Vp does not throw: it returns every field as NaN, and a panel shows the gap. The halfspace message belongs to zoeppritzRpp and the positivity message to substituteVels; no engine clamps Vs to Vp.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-elastic-logs-at-the-wells', 2, 'A learner fits the local shear trend, linear, on only three measured Vp and Vs pairs. What does fitVsRegression do?', '["It refuses, in the engine''s own words: \"Need at least 5 samples with measured Vp and Vs (got 3).\"", "It refuses, in the engine''s own words: \"The regression is singular (Vp has no spread).\"", "It returns a line through the three pairs with a residual standard error of zero and an R squared of one.", "It returns NaN coefficients, the way elasticPoint returns NaN fields when its input has no physical answer."]'::jsonb, 0, 'fitVsRegression given three measured pairs in the linear form refuses with the message about five samples, and the panel prints it unchanged. The singular message comes from six pairs all at Vp 3000 m/s, and a NaN result is elasticPoint''s behaviour, which the fit does not share.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-elastic-logs-at-the-wells', 3, 'In the feasibility explorer the net sand Vsh cut-off is moved up and down from 0.350000 at Ekene-1. Which layer''s sample count stays at its Ekene figure, and why?', '["The oil leg''s 65 samples, because the oil-water contact at 5118.110236 ft fixes the leg whatever the cut-off.", "The water leg''s 103 samples, because every sample below the contact is counted whatever its shale volume reads.", "The net sand''s 168 samples, because the cut-off is applied after the count and only reweights the layer means.", "The cap rock''s 130 samples, because it is the 65 ft of log above the top of the Ekene Sand and has no Vsh rule."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Net sand is Vsh at or below the stated cut-off, and the oil leg (65) and water leg (103) are net sand above and below the contact, so both move with it. The cap rock is a stated thickness of log, the 65 ft above the top, 130 samples, set by its own stated thickness.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-elastic-logs-at-the-wells', 4, 'Over the 103 water-leg net sand samples of Ekene-1, what is the mean of measured Vs over the Greenberg-Castagna Vs?', '["1.151143, the mean over the oil-leg samples, where the oil has softened the Vp the regression is given.", "1.036576, so on average the measured shear sits above what the published regression predicts.", "0.925483, the R squared of the local shear trend fitted to the same 103 water-leg samples by least squares.", "1.062182, the ratio at 5144.000000 ft, the largest of the six tabled samples, read as the mean of the leg."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The mean of measured over Greenberg-Castagna over the 103 water-leg samples is 1.036576, above one, so the regression gives too little shear for this sand. 1.151143 is the oil leg, 0.925483 is a fit statistic of the local trend, and 1.062182 is one sample.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-elastic-logs-at-the-wells', 5, 'Measured over Greenberg-Castagna Vs is 1.036576 in the Ekene-1 water leg. What is it over the 65 oil-leg samples, and what does the course say drives it?', '["1.036576 again, because the published coefficients describe the same sandstone in both legs of the Ekene Sand.", "1.151143, because the oil softens Vp and leaves the shear modulus alone.", "0.925483, because the oil stiffens the shear modulus and pulls the measured shear below the regression.", "1.151143, because the oil leg is cleaner sand, so the engine applies the shale coefficients 0.769690 and -0.867350."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Over the 65 oil-leg samples the ratio is 1.151143: the oil softens Vp and leaves the shear modulus alone, so a regression fed that lower Vp predicts less shear than the rock carries. The oil leg is the shalier leg (Vsh 0.178134 against 0.117854), and the engine mixes the two lithologies on Vsh.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-elastic-logs-at-the-wells', 6, 'How does gcSandShaleVs predict shear for an Ekene sample with some shale in it?', '["From the sandstone regression alone (a1 0.804160, a0 -0.855880) whenever the sample''s Vsh is at or below the 0.350000 net sand cut-off.", "From the local trend fitted at the well (c0 -970.382459, c1 855.280029), which the engine substitutes for the published coefficients once a shear log exists.", "From the shale regression alone (a1 0.769690, a0 -0.867350) in m/s, because any clay in the sample lowers its shear toward the shale line.", "From the sandstone (a1 0.804160, a0 -0.855880) and shale (a1 0.769690, a0 -0.867350) regressions in km/s, mixed on Vsh as the average of their arithmetic and harmonic means."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Greenberg-Castagna is a published regression per lithology, Vs = a2 Vp^2 + a1 Vp + a0 in km/s, and a mixed sample is weighted on Vsh as the average of the arithmetic and harmonic means of the two predictions. The local trend is a separate fit, fitVsRegression, and the net sand cut-off picks samples; it does not pick coefficients.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-elastic-logs-at-the-wells', 7, 'What slope does the linear local shear trend return on the 103 water-leg net sand samples of Ekene-1?', '["855.280029 m/s of Vs per km/s of Vp.", "872.045751, the four-well slope.", "3017.560894, the quadratic c1.", "0.804160, the sandstone a1."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The linear fit on Ekene-1''s water leg gives c1 855.280029 m/s per km/s with c0 -970.382459 m/s. Fitting all four wells (465 samples) moves the slope to 872.045751, the quadratic''s c1 3017.560894 belongs to a different form with a c2 term, and 0.804160 is the published sandstone regression.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-elastic-logs-at-the-wells', 8, 'At the 0.900000 level, predictVs is asked for Vs at Vp 3700 m/s on the Ekene-1 water-leg trend. What does it return?', '["A refusal, because a Vp outside the calibration range of 2670.381000 to 3481.638000 m/s has no prediction interval the engine can compute.", "Vs 1852.041638 m/s, the prediction at the highest Vp the engine will use, since it holds every Vp above the range at the last calibrated value.", "Vs 2194.153650 m/s, sigma 43.298759 m/s, flagged as extrapolated because 3700 m/s lies above the highest calibrated Vp of 3481.638000 m/s.", "Vs 2194.153650 m/s with sigma 41.009437 m/s, unflagged, because an R squared of 0.925483 keeps the line trusted beyond its samples."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A Vp outside the calibration range still gets a prediction, but it is flagged as extrapolated and its sigma is 43.298759 m/s. The engine neither refuses nor clamps, and sigma is s sqrt(1 + leverage), so it is not the fit''s s of 41.009437 m/s.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-elastic-logs-at-the-wells', 9, 'The prediction interval at Vp 3100 m/s is 1612.548122 to 1749.423143 m/s at the 0.900000 level. What does the engine return at the 0.8 level?', '["1612.548122 to 1749.423143 m/s, unchanged.", "1615.915396 to 1746.630980 m/s.", "A wider interval than at 0.900000, because a lower level loosens the fit and so raises the residual standard error.", "1627.805269 to 1734.165996 m/s, a narrower interval."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The level is a stated choice. At 0.8 the interval at 3100 m/s is 1627.805269 to 1734.165996 m/s, narrower than at 0.900000. 1615.915396 to 1746.630980 m/s is the four-well fit at 0.900000, and the level does not touch s.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-elastic-logs-at-the-wells', 10, 'A learner passes fitVsRegression six measured pairs, every one at Vp 3000 m/s. What happens?', '["The engine refuses: \"The regression is singular (Vp has no spread).\"", "The engine refuses: \"Need at least 5 samples with measured Vp and Vs (got 3).\", the count check every fit runs first.", "It drops the repeats and fits one pair.", "It returns every coefficient as NaN."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Six pairs pass the count check, but with no spread in Vp the least squares system is singular and the engine refuses with that message. The count message needs fewer than five pairs, and the fit returns no NaN result in place of a refusal.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-elastic-logs-at-the-wells', 11, 'The local shear trend at Ekene-1 is refitted in the quadratic form on the same 103 samples. What changes?', '["s falls to 39.612304 m/s and the prediction at 3100 m/s becomes 1681.273188 m/s, as the extra coefficient takes up curvature.", "s rises above 41.009437 m/s with the extra term.", "s falls from 41.009437 to 39.004155 m/s, and the prediction at Vp 3100 m/s becomes 1690.797241 m/s.", "Nothing: 1680.985632 m/s at 3100 m/s."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The form is a stated choice. The quadratic gives c0 -4335.156360, c1 3017.560894, c2 -346.356417, s 39.004155 m/s and 1690.797241 m/s at 3100 m/s; the extra coefficient buys a smaller s. 39.612304 and 1681.273188 are the four-well linear fit.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-elastic-logs-at-the-wells', 12, 'Which sigma comes back beside the Vs predicted for 3100 m/s from the Ekene-1 water-leg fit?', '["41.009437 m/s, the fit''s s alone.", "41.225414 m/s, s times sqrt(1 + leverage).", "43.298759 m/s, the 3700 sigma.", "39.612304 m/s, the four-well s."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Sigma is s sqrt(1 + leverage), so it sits above the fit''s scatter s of 41.009437 m/s: 41.225414 at 3100 m/s, 41.646413 at 2900 and 43.298759 at the extrapolated 3700. The level enters through t in the interval, and 39.612304 belongs to a different fit.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-elastic-logs-at-the-wells', 13, 'What AI (acoustic impedance) does elasticPoint return for the Ekene-1 oil leg, run on the layer''s mean Vp, Vs and density?', '["6639018.603084 kg/(m2 s).", "7358819.677147 kg/(m2 s), the water leg''s AI, which elasticPoint returns for the whole net sand of the Ekene Sand.", "3795337.170439, its SI.", "4697628.723902, cap rock."]'::jsonb, 0, 'AI is Vp times density, and on the oil leg''s means (2865.750738 m/s and 2316.676923 kg/m3) elasticPoint returns 6639018.603084 kg/(m2 s). 3795337.170439 is the oil leg''s SI, Vs times density; 7358819.677147 is the water leg and 4697628.723902 the cap rock.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-elastic-logs-at-the-wells', 14, 'At Ekene-1 the cap rock''s AI (acoustic impedance) is 4697628.723902 kg/(m2 s) and both legs read higher. On the kit''s SEG normal polarity, what is the top of the Ekene Sand?', '["A trough, because the shale''s Vp/Vs of 2.916045 stands far above both legs and the reflection follows Vp/Vs.", "A trough, set by density.", "No reflection, because the shale''s lambda-rho of 16.877337 sits between the oil leg''s and the water leg''s lambda-rho.", "A peak, an increase in impedance downwards."]'::jsonb, 3, 'On SEG normal polarity an increase in acoustic impedance downwards is a peak, and the Ekene Sand is harder than the Ogbia Shale at Ekene-1 (AI 6639018.603084 and 7358819.677147 against 4697628.723902). The sign follows AI; Vp/Vs, density alone and lambda-rho do not set it.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-elastic-logs-at-the-wells', 15, 'At Ekene-1 the oil leg is softer than the water leg in AI (acoustic impedance) and Vp/Vs. What does the course say that gap is made of?', '["Fluid alone, because both legs are net sand under the same 0.350000 cut-off and so share one rock frame throughout.", "Rock alone, because mu-rho differs between the legs (14.404584 against 16.213500) and a pore fluid leaves mu-rho unchanged.", "Part fluid and part rock: the oil leg has porosity 0.203626 and Vsh 0.178134, the water leg 0.197677 and 0.117854.", "Fluid alone, because the water leg is the shalier sand and its clay and the brine in it cancel each other out."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The legs are different net sand: the oil leg''s porosity and Vsh are 0.203626 and 0.178134, the water leg''s 0.197677 and 0.117854, so comparing them mixes fluid and rock. The water leg is the cleaner leg, a shared cut-off does not make one rock, and substituting brine into the same oil-leg rock is how the next module separates the two.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-fluid-show', 1, 'At the stated Ekene conditions (180 degF, 3200 psia, 32 API, gas-oil ratio 400 scf/stb), what bulk modulus does liveOil return?', '["2.662550 GPa, the stiffer of the two fluids, which is what the brine function returns at the same conditions.", "0.852881 GPa.", "0.865443, the oil''s reference density in g/cc from apiToRho0, read off the table as if it were a modulus.", "1.398867 GPa, the pore fluid''s modulus at the oil-leg mean Sw of 0.574254."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Batzle-Wang at the stated conditions gives the live oil a bulk modulus of 0.852881 GPa, density 754.214377 kg/m3 and Vp 1063.400328 m/s. 2.662550 GPa is the brine, 0.865443 g/cc is a density, and 1.398867 GPa is the Wood mix of the two at the oil-leg mean Sw.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-fluid-show', 2, 'woodMix mixes the Ekene brine and live oil at Sw 0.500000. Which result does it return, and what does it show?', '["2.662550 GPa, because any brine in the pore space sets the modulus of the mix to the stiffer phase as soon as it is present.", "879.708973 GPa, the mix at half saturation, read straight from the woodMix table''s other column, the one that climbs in even steps as Sw rises.", "0.987056 GPa, the halfway point between the two fluids, since Wood''s average weights each modulus by its volume fraction.", "1.291926 GPa, which sits much nearer the oil''s 0.852881 GPa than the brine''s 2.662550 GPa: the Wood average stays close to the softer phase."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At Sw 0.500000 Wood''s average returns 1.291926 GPa, far nearer the oil than the brine: the Wood average sits close to the softer phase until the brine fraction is high. 879.708973 is the mix''s density in kg/m3, and 0.987056 GPa is the mix at Sw 0.200000.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-fluid-show', 3, 'The lab converts the stated Ekene pressure of 3200 psia before calling Batzle-Wang. What does the brine function receive?', '["22.063223 MPa, using 1 MPa = 145.037738 psi.", "82.222222 MPa, the converted temperature figure.", "145.037738 MPa, the conversion factor the lab divides by, passed on in place of the pressure.", "71.243043 MPa, read from the converted gas-oil ratio, which the lab hands to the brine engine in litres per litre."]'::jsonb, 0, '3200 psia over 145.037738 psi per MPa is 22.063223 MPa. 82.222222 is the temperature in degC, 71.243043 is the gas-oil ratio in L/L for the oil, and 145.037738 is the factor itself.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-fluid-show', 4, 'A learner sets the pressure to 0 in the feasibility explorer and calls brine. What does the panel print?', '["The engine''s own words: \"Salinity must be a weight fraction in [0, 0.5).\"", "A brine row with every field NaN and the gap shown, as elasticPoint does.", "The engine''s own words: \"Temperature (degC) and positive pressure (MPa) required.\"", "The engine''s own words: \"API gravity must be in (0, 100).\", since the oil and the brine share one check on the conditions."]'::jsonb, 2, 'brine refuses a pressure of 0 MPa with the message on temperature and positive pressure. The salinity message comes from a salinity of 0.6 and the API message from apiToRho0 at 0 API; a NaN result belongs to elasticPoint with Vs above Vp.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-fluid-show', 5, 'Oil-to-brine substitution at 5082.000000 ft at Ekene-1 takes Vs from 1358.537000 to 1349.137957 m/s. What does the course say the method holds fixed?', '["The shear modulus; the density swaps the pore fluid, so Vs falls a little as the rock gets denser.", "Vs itself, so the small fall is rounding in the table.", "The density, so the whole Vs change comes from brine adding shear stiffness to the frame that the oil had taken away.", "The pore fluid''s bulk modulus, which the method keeps at its in situ value, so every velocity change is a density change alone."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Gassmann substitution leaves the shear modulus unchanged and swaps the pore fluid in the density, rho_new = rho + phi (rho_brine - rho_fluid): at 5082.000000 ft density rises from 2311.300000 to 2343.616460 kg/m3 and Vs falls slightly. The pore fluid''s modulus does change, from 1.189766 GPa in situ to brine.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-fluid-show', 6, 'At 5082.000000 ft (Vsh 0.325800) the substitution''s mineral modulus is 30.444339 GPa. Where does that figure come from?', '["Quartz alone at 36.600000 GPa, softened by the porosity of 0.220700.", "The in situ pore fluid of 1.189766 GPa added to the clay''s 20.900000 GPa as the frame takes on the oil and brine in it.", "The clean sample at 5114.000000 ft, whose 35.087723 GPa the lab applies to every oil-leg depth, scaled by its Vsh.", "Quartz (K 36.600000 GPa) and clay (K 20.900000 GPa) mixed by Voigt-Reuss-Hill on the sample''s Vsh."]'::jsonb, 3, 'mixMinerals mixes quartz (36.600000 GPa) and clay (20.900000 GPa) by Voigt-Reuss-Hill on each sample''s Vsh, so the shaly sample at 5082.000000 ft carries 30.444339 GPa and the clean one at 5114.000000 ft 35.087723 GPa. The pore fluid, 1.189766 GPa here, enters Gassmann separately.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-fluid-show', 7, 'A learner passes substituteVels a porosity of 0. What happens?', '["It returns the in situ Vp unchanged, since with no pore space there is no fluid for brine to replace in the rock.", "It refuses: \"Porosity must be in (0, 1).\"", "It refuses: \"vp, vs and rho must be positive.\"", "It refuses: \"Fractions must sum to 1.\", the check mixMinerals makes on the quartz and clay fractions it is given."]'::jsonb, 1, 'substituteVels refuses a porosity of 0 with the porosity message, in the engine''s own words. The positivity message answers a negative Vp, and the fractions message is mixMinerals given quartz 0.7 and clay 0.2. The engine returns no unchanged Vp in place of a refusal.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-fluid-show', 8, 'Over the 65 oil-leg samples of Ekene-1, how far does taking the leg to brine move the mean AI (acoustic impedance)?', '["Up 7.142745 percent, the same move the leg''s mean Vp/Vs makes when brine replaces the oil.", "Down 7.456776 percent, because brine is denser and the heavier pore fluid slows the compressional wave through the rock.", "Up 7.456776 percent, from 6641244.924742 to 7136467.682134 kg/(m2 s).", "Up to 7358819.677147 kg/(m2 s), the water leg''s figure."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The mean of the sample values of AI rises 7.456776 percent, 6641244.924742 to 7136467.682134 kg/(m2 s): Vp and density both rise when brine replaces the oil. 7.142745 percent is the Vp/Vs change, and 7358819.677147 is the measured water leg, which is different rock.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-fluid-show', 9, 'The Ekene-1 oil leg taken to brine reads a mean AI (acoustic impedance) of 7136467.682134 kg/(m2 s); the measured water leg reads 7358819.677147. What does the course say about that gap?', '["The substitution is undercooked: brine has not fully replaced the oil.", "It is residual oil left in the water leg below the contact at 5118.110236 ft, which lowers that leg''s measured impedance.", "The two should agree, so the gap is the error of Gassmann and a study corrects every substituted figure up to the water leg.", "The two are different rock: the substituted figure is the fluid effect alone, and the water leg adds the difference in porosity and clay."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The substituted oil leg keeps its own rock (porosity 0.203626, Vsh 0.178134) and changes only the fluid; the water leg is different rock (0.197677, 0.117854). So the substituted figures are the fluid effect alone and the water-leg figures add porosity and clay. The water leg''s AI is the higher one, so no oil lowers it.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-fluid-show', 10, 'For the Ekene-1 oil leg in situ, the course prints an AI (acoustic impedance) of 6641244.924742 and one of 6639018.603084 kg/(m2 s). How do they differ?', '["6641244.924742 is the mean of the 65 sample values; 6639018.603084 is elasticPoint on the layer''s mean Vp and density.", "They are one figure, printed twice at six decimals, and the gap between them is rounding carried through two different routes through the lab.", "6639018.603084 is the mean of the sample values and 6641244.924742 is elasticPoint on the layer''s means.", "6641244.924742 is the oil leg after substitution to brine, and 6639018.603084 is the same leg as it is."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Two kinds of mean appear: the substitution table averages each sample''s AI to 6641244.924742, while the elastic set averages Vp, Vs and density first and computes AI from the means, 6639018.603084. Taken to brine the leg''s mean of the sample values reads 7136467.682134.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-fluid-show', 11, 'How does fitFluidLine fit the wet trend to the brine-substituted block boundaries?', '["By ordinary least squares of B on A with a free intercept, so the line need not pass through the origin of the crossplot.", "By total least squares through the origin, the principal axis of the (A, B) points, so neither A nor B is treated as free of error.", "By the published Castagna, Swan and Foster slope, computed from the blocks'' mean Vs/Vp and a Gardner exponent and then drawn through the points.", "By ordinary least squares of B on A through the origin, fitted to the in situ blocks before any sample is taken to brine."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The wet trend is the fluid line through the origin fitted by total least squares to the intercepts and gradients of block boundaries after every sample with oil (79 of 800 at Ekene-1) is taken to brine. The Castagna, Swan and Foster slope is the published line, a separate comparison.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-fluid-show', 12, 'On Ekene-1 with 16 ft blocks, the fitted wet trend has slope -2.257963. What does the published Castagna, Swan and Foster line give?', '["-66.112544, the published line''s angle.", "-2.565814, since the published line is the fitted line at the 8 ft blocks the publication assumes for every well.", "-0.404345, at the blocks'' mean Vs/Vp of 0.408987 and a Gardner exponent of 0.25.", "-2.257963 as well, because the published line is calibrated to the same blocked logs and so lands on the fitted slope."]'::jsonb, 2, 'backgroundSlope assumes one Vs/Vp for every boundary and returns -0.404345 at Vs/Vp 0.408987 and g 0.25, far shallower than the fitted -2.257963. -66.112544 degrees is the fitted line''s angle and -2.565814 is the fitted slope at 8 ft blocks.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-fluid-show', 13, 'At Ekene-1, what happens to the fitted wet trend when the stated block length is cut from 16 ft to 8 ft?', '["49 interfaces in place of 24, and the slope steepens to -2.565814.", "16 interfaces, and the slope settles at -2.254272.", "The engine refuses: \"A fluid line needs at least three interfaces.\", because shorter blocks leave fewer boundaries.", "Nothing in the slope: it stays -2.257963, since the block length only changes how many points the crossplot draws."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The block length is a stated choice: 8 ft blocks (16 samples) give 49 interfaces and slope -2.565814, with rms distance 0.006424. 16 interfaces and -2.254272 belong to 24 ft blocks, and the three-interface refusal answers a fit given two interfaces, the opposite end.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-fluid-show', 14, 'Against the fitted wet trend, the top of the Ekene Sand at Ekene-1 sits at -0.072091 in situ and -0.004519 with brine. How does the course read that pair?', '["The brine top is the anomaly, because -0.004519 lies on the positive side of a falling trend line where wet rock should not sit.", "Both lie inside the trend''s own scatter, an rms distance of 0.006806, so no fluid effect can be read from the pair.", "The oil moves the top 0.067572 toward the positive side, away from the side a hydrocarbon sand moves to on a falling line.", "The oil moves the top 0.067572 further to the hydrocarbon side."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Negative is the hydrocarbon side. The brine top sits almost on the trend at -0.004519, and the same rock with oil sits at -0.072091, 0.067572 further negative. The rock is the same in both rows, so the oil moves the point off the trend.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-fluid-show', 15, 'Measured from the published line, the Ekene-1 top reads -0.458460 in situ and -0.363599 with brine. What does that show?', '["The published line is the correct background, so the brine top is a strong fluid anomaly and the oil leg is wet.", "The line a distance is read from is a stated choice: both tops read far negative from the published line, and the oil top still lies further to the negative side.", "The two lines give the same distance once the sign is dropped, so a verdict may quote either one without naming it.", "The oil moves the top toward the positive side when it is read from the published line, the reverse of what the fitted line shows."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The two lines give different distances: from the fitted line the tops read -0.072091 and -0.004519, from the published line -0.458460 and -0.363599. Which line the distance is read from is a stated choice, and a verdict states it beside the figure.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-feasibility-verdict', 1, 'The modelled top of the Ekene Sand at Ekene-1 has A 0.170742 in situ and 0.205564 with brine. What class does avoClass return for each, and what follows?', '["Class II in situ and class I with brine, because the oil softens the sand and pulls its intercept down into the band around zero.", "Class III in situ, since its gradient of -0.563558 is at or below zero, and class I with brine.", "Class I for both, since both intercepts sit above 0.02, so the class alone does not separate oil from brine here.", "Class I in situ and class IV with brine, because the brine curve turns up again by 35 degrees."]'::jsonb, 2, 'avoClass calls A above 0.02 class I, whatever B is, and both intercepts (0.170742 and 0.205564) clear that band, so both tops are class I. Class III needs A more than 0.02 below zero, and the brine curve rising to 0.166006 at 35 degrees does not change a class read from A and B.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-feasibility-verdict', 2, 'Under avoClass, an interface whose intercept A lies more than 0.02 below zero and whose gradient B is above zero falls in which class?', '["Class IV.", "Class III, the class of every interface with A more than 0.02 below zero, whatever its gradient reads.", "Class II, because any gradient above zero pulls the interface back into the band around zero intercept.", "Class I, because a positive gradient brightens the reflection with angle the way a hard sand''s top does."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The rule is: A above 0.02 class I; A within 0.02 of zero class II; otherwise class III when B is at or below zero and class IV when B is above it. A below the band with B above zero is class IV.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-feasibility-verdict', 3, 'How much dimmer is the modelled in situ top of the Ekene Sand than the same rock with brine, at normal incidence and at 30 degrees?', '["The oil brightens the top: the in situ coefficient sits above the brine one by 0.034809 at normal incidence.", "By 0.066214 at normal incidence, shrinking to 0.034809 at 30 degrees as the two curves converge.", "By 0.034809 at normal incidence, with no gap left by 30 degrees, where the two exact curves cross over.", "By 0.034809 at normal incidence and 0.066214 at 30 degrees, below the brine coefficient at every angle."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The exact coefficient with oil is below the brine one throughout: 0.171249 against 0.206058 at 0 degrees, a gap of 0.034809, and 0.070998 against 0.137212 at 30 degrees, 0.066214. The gap widens with angle, and the curves never cross in the table.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-feasibility-verdict', 4, 'Which exact plane-wave coefficient does zoeppritzRpp give at normal incidence for the oil-bearing case?', '["0.170742, Shuey''s intercept A for the same interface.", "0.171249.", "0.206058, read from the brine column of the same table and taken for the oil case.", "0.156172, the near mean over 5 to 15 degrees."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The exact plane-wave coefficient at normal incidence is 0.171249 in situ. Shuey''s A of 0.170742 comes from a different engine call and sits close to it without being the same figure; 0.206058 is the brine top and 0.156172 the near stack mean.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-feasibility-verdict', 5, 'What far mean does the model return for the in situ top over 25 to 35 degrees?', '["0.073416, the mean exact coefficient at every whole degree from 25 to 35, ends included.", "0.070998, the exact coefficient at 30 degrees, the middle of the range, which the model takes as the stack''s value.", "0.060228, the exact coefficient at 35 degrees, the far end of the range, where the far stack takes its value.", "0.144237, the far mean of the same range, which the model reports for the in situ top."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Each stack mean averages the exact coefficient at every whole degree of its range, ends included, the way the kit built its angle stacks: 0.073416 in situ over 25 to 35 degrees. 0.070998 and 0.060228 are single angles, and 0.144237 is the brine top''s far mean.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-feasibility-verdict', 6, 'What two layers does the modelled AVO at the top of the Ekene Sand use?', '["The mean oil leg over the mean water leg, the contact at 5118.110236 ft read as the interface that the seismic sees.", "The blocked logs of the 16 ft wet trend, convolved with a wavelet so the model carries the thickness of the sand.", "Two half-spaces: the mean cap rock over the mean oil leg in situ, and the same cap rock over the oil leg taken to brine.", "The mean cap rock over the measured water leg, which stands in for the oil leg with brine."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The model is two half-spaces, the mean cap rock over the mean oil leg as it is and over the oil leg''s substituted means, with no wavelet and no thickness. The measured water leg is different rock (porosity 0.197677 against 0.203626), so it cannot stand in for the same rock with brine.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-feasibility-verdict', 7, 'A learner models a reflection with an upper half-space whose shear velocity exceeds its compressional velocity. What happens?', '["A coefficient of NaN at every angle, the way elasticPoint returns NaN fields for the same impossible layer.", "The message \"vp, vs and rho must be positive.\"", "A curve computed with the upper Vs clamped at its Vp, flagged in the panel as extrapolated.", "The engine''s own words: \"Upper halfspace has vs >= vp.\""]'::jsonb, 3, 'zoeppritzRpp refuses an upper layer with Vs above Vp, and the panel prints that message unchanged. The NaN result is elasticPoint''s behaviour, the positivity message is substituteVels, and no engine clamps Vs; extrapolated is predictVs''s flag.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-feasibility-verdict', 8, 'Who or what decides whether a QI study at Ekene should go ahead?', '["avoClass, whose class I at the top of the Ekene Sand is the engine''s go decision for the study, in situ and with brine.", "The interpreter, who writes the verdict from the numbers the engines return.", "QI Studio''s usability matrix, which grades nine Ekene wells limited and one missing, and so decides the study for the team.", "The capstone, which grades a verdict alongside its six figures."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engines return spectra, trends, moduli and reflection coefficients; they do not decide whether a QI study should go ahead, and the verdict is written by the interpreter. A class is never graded, the usability grades are never graded, and no verdict is graded.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-feasibility-verdict', 9, 'A verdict quotes the in situ top at -0.072091 from the wet trend. Which choices must it state beside that figure?', '["The block length of the wet trend and the line the distance is read from, with the selections and the fluids and conditions.", "None, because the fitted wet trend is fixed by the well''s logs and every reader of the verdict would rebuild the same line.", "The maximum lag of the signal to noise.", "The taper of the spectrum alone, because the wet trend is fitted to the seismic spectrum and inherits its band from it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A verdict states its selections, its fluids and conditions, its taper and windows, its block length and the line the distance is read from, because each one moves a figure. The distance rests on the block length and the line; the lag and the taper belong to the data question.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-feasibility-verdict', 10, 'The verdict''s band row asks whether the band is wide enough at the target, and it is read from the 800 to 1600 ms window. What ties that window to the question?', '["It is the only window the spread traces hold.", "QI Studio fixes it for every spectrum, so no other window can be stated.", "The deep window takes no taper.", "The window holds the Ekene Sand, the target the row asks about."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The spectrum window 800 to 1600 ms holds the Ekene Sand, so the row quotes that window, and a verdict states its windows because each one moves a figure. The spread traces run 800 to 2400 ms, the course tables three windows, and every one of them is measured at taper 0.100000.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-feasibility-verdict', 11, 'What does the verdict quote for whether the signal is clean enough at Ekene?', '["A median of 77.008444, 18.865384 dB, the figure for the window that holds the Ekene Sand in the neighbour traces.", "A median of 999998.999971, which shows the neighbouring traces are identical and the data are noise-free at the target.", "A median signal to noise of 361.155122 as a power ratio, 25.576938 dB, along the inline through Ekene-1.", "A ratio of 25.576938 and a decibel figure of 361.155122."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Signal to noise is a power ratio from trace coherency: the median over 20 pairs of the 800 to 1600 ms window is 361.155122, 25.576938 dB. 77.008444 is the 1600 to 2400 ms window, 999998.999971 is the ceiling at the clamp 0.999999, and 25.576938 is the decibel form, so reading it as the ratio swaps the two.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-feasibility-verdict', 12, 'The verdict''s footprint row reads "share 0.015148 along the crosslines, no stripe by the rule". Which data does that row rest on?', '["The full-stack spread traces of 800 to 1600 ms, the same window and traces the band row and its spectrum are read from.", "The RMS amplitude slice of the full stack, 1250 to 1350 ms, 128 inlines by 128 crosslines, with periods searched from 2 to 16 lines.", "The designed teaching case with every sixth crossline multiplied by 1.2, the slice on which the search finds its stripe and the one the verdict reports.", "Signed full-stack amplitudes at 1288.796378 ms, the two-way time of the top of the Ekene Sand at Ekene-1."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Ekene slice is the RMS amplitude of the full stack, 1250 to 1350 ms, 128 by 128 lines, searched over periods of 2 to 16 lines. The designed stripe is a teaching case the course adds and labels as such, and signed seismic is avoided because a stripe of gain averages away on it.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-feasibility-verdict', 13, 'Which of these does the course say is never graded?', '["The class of the case''s top interface, which the panel prints from A and B and the capstone marks as one of its six figures.", "The distance of the case''s top interface from its own fitted wet trend, the figure that answers the fluid question.", "The slope of the local shear trend on the case''s water-leg net sand, since it rests on a stated form of fit.", "Any verdict, any class name and any Ekene figure."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Never graded: any figure in the videos, any Ekene figure, any verdict, class name or category, and any figure that depends on a choice the case does not state. The distance from the case''s own fitted wet trend and the shear slope on the case''s water-leg net sand are two of the Associate capstone''s six graded figures.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-feasibility-verdict', 14, 'In the Associate capstone, which line is the case''s top-interface distance measured from?', '["The case''s own fitted wet trend.", "The published Castagna, Swan and Foster line, computed from the case''s mean Vs/Vp and a Gardner exponent.", "The Ekene-1 fitted wet trend of slope -2.257963, which the panel keeps as the reference line for every case.", "Any line the learner states."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The capstone grades the distance of the case''s top interface from its own fitted wet trend, with every input it depends on stated in the case file. The published line gives a different distance, Ekene is never the case, and a figure that depends on an unstated choice is never graded.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-feasibility-verdict', 15, 'At the modelled top of the Ekene Sand, how do Shuey''s intercept and gradient move when the oil leg is taken from in situ to brine?', '["A falls from 0.205564 to 0.170742 and B goes from -0.475316 to the more negative -0.563558, oil to brine.", "A stays at 0.170742, since the intercept depends on the rock frame alone, while B moves to -0.475316 with the brine.", "A rises from 0.170742 to 0.205564, and B goes from -0.563558 to the less negative -0.475316.", "A rises to 0.205564 and B becomes more negative at -0.563558, because the stiffer brine steepens the gradient."]'::jsonb, 2, 'With oil the top reads A 0.170742 and B -0.563558; with brine A 0.205564 and B -0.475316. So the oil leaves A lower and B more negative, and taking the leg to brine reverses both.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 1, 'A learner hunts for the Ekene-1 top on the seismic at 644.398189 ms and finds it above the window the band and signal figures were measured in. What has gone wrong?', '["Nothing: the band and signal windows were chosen too deep, and the study should remeasure them from a shallower start.", "The learner read the top in metres, 1548.000000, where the course works in feet, and a feet figure lands lower on the trace.", "The checkshot gives one-way time; doubled, the top sits at TWT 1288.796378 ms, inside the 800 to 1600 ms window of those figures.", "The top really is above the window, because the Ekene Sand is shallower than the 800 ms start of the spread traces."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The spread traces run 800 to 2400 ms, and the -6 dB band of 14.425434 to 34.787627 Hz and the median 361.155122 are read in 800 to 1600 ms, the window holding the sand; the RMS slice spans 1250 to 1350 ms.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 2, 'At 0 degrees the modelled Ekene-1 top has an exact coefficient of 0.171249 with oil and 0.206058 with brine. On the kit''s seismic, what sign does the event carry in each case?', '["Positive in both, a peak whose strength the oil reduces.", "Negative with oil and positive with brine, since oil turns the sand softer than the Ogbia Shale above it.", "Negative in both, because SEG normal polarity prints an increase in impedance downwards as a trough on the trace.", "Positive with oil and negative with brine."]'::jsonb, 0, 'SEG normal polarity prints an impedance increase downwards as a positive number. Both coefficients are positive; the oil lowers the peak by 0.034809 without changing its sign.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 3, 'The Ekene-1 oil leg''s mu-rho is printed as 14.404584. In what unit, and from what is it built?', '["In GPa alone, as the shear modulus mu = rho Vs^2 read straight from the logs without any density factor.", "In kg/(m2 s), since mu-rho is the shear impedance, Vs times density, printed in the units of AI.", "In units of 10^6 kg/(m2 s), as AI squared less twice SI squared, the Goodway, Chen and Downton lambda term.", "In 10^12 kg^2/(m4 s2), which is (GPa)(g/cc); it is SI squared, SI being Vs times density."]'::jsonb, 3, 'After Goodway, Chen and Downton, mu-rho = SI^2 and lambda-rho = AI^2 - 2 SI^2. The oil-leg SI is 3795337.170439 kg/(m2 s), and its lambda-rho prints as 15.267400.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 4, 'A depth of 1548.000000 turns up in a table of the top of the Ekene Sand at Ekene-1. What has happened?', '["It is a two-way time in milliseconds that was pasted into the depth column of the table by mistake.", "It is the kit''s figure in metres; the course prints the same top as 5078.740157 ft, feet being metres / 0.3048.", "It is the true vertical depth of a deviated well, which reads shallower than the measured depth of the same top.", "It is the depth below sea level, the measured depth less the KB elevation of 82.020997 ft."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The kit writes metres and the course converts: 1548.000000 m is 5078.740157 ft. The pack wells are vertical, so no deviation correction applies.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 5, 'The same defect, a Vs above Vp, reaches elasticPoint in one layer and zoeppritzRpp as the upper layer of a model. What do the two engines do?', '["Both throw, because every Associate engine checks that Vs sits below Vp before it computes anything at all.", "Both return NaN fields, so the panel shows a gap in the elastic set and a matching gap in the angle table.", "elasticPoint hands back a row of NaN fields without throwing; zoeppritzRpp throws a refusal naming the upper half-space.", "elasticPoint throws, and zoeppritzRpp returns zero."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A result with NaN fields is a result and no refusal: AI and Vp/Vs come back as NaN and the panel shows the gap. zoeppritzRpp is among the 21 refusals across 14 functions.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 6, 'Why does no capstone of this course use the Ekene field as its case?', '["The members-only videos speak the Ekene answers aloud, so a graded Ekene figure could be answered by watching a video.", "The feasibility explorer runs the engines only on a pasted case, so the Ekene pack never reaches the panel that is graded.", "The Ekene pack is too small for the engines to run on, so each capstone ships a larger case built for the panel.", "The Ekene figures change with every release of the kit, so a graded answer would drift between cohorts."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Each tier grades six numeric fields, each a return value of an engine on a held-out synthetic case the learner pastes into the panel. Ekene is synthetic too, cut from release ekene-demo-v3.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 7, 'A learner types a pressure of 0 into the fluids view. The stated Ekene pressure is 3200 psia. What does the brine engine do with each?', '["It accepts 0 and returns the brine at surface conditions, and it reads the stated 3200 as MPa without any conversion.", "It refuses both, since Batzle-Wang takes pressure in psia and the panel passes the typed figure straight through.", "It clamps 0 to the lowest pressure it accepts and returns brine at 1005.203569 kg/m3 for both entries.", "It refuses 0, since it needs a positive pressure, and runs 3200 psia as 22.063223 MPa."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engines work in SI, so the panel converts psia to MPa at 145.037738 psi to the MPa. At the stated conditions the brine reads 1005.203569 kg/m3 and 2.662550 GPa.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 8, 'Ekene-9 carries a sonic, a shear sonic and 24 checkshot levels, yet its usability cell is missing. Why?', '["Its deviation survey is absent, and an absent survey is missing because a deviated well cannot be placed in depth without one.", "Density is a required item and it is absent, and a cell takes the worst of its items; AI is Vp times density, so no impedance can be formed there.", "Its shear item is missing, because a deviated well''s shear sonic is never accepted in place of a Greenberg-Castagna estimate.", "Its checkshots are too few."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Sonic and density are required; Ekene-9''s curves are CALI, GR, DT, DTS and RT, and its density item reads "No density over the zone." Its survey and checkshots grade good.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 9, 'The headers cover 12 wells but the usability matrix has only 10 cells. Which wells are left out, and why?', '["Ekene-8 and Ekene-9, because a deviated well is graded only after its deviation survey has been checked by hand.", "Ekene-9 and Ekene-10, the two wells whose curve lists differ from the 11 curves the other logged wells carry.", "Ekene-1 and Ekene-4, the wells whose curves the pack replaces with the kit''s own PHIT, VSH and SW.", "Ekene-11, which is planned, and Ekene-12, a survey location; neither has logs."]'::jsonb, 3, '10 of 12 wells have logs. Ekene-11 is planned and Ekene-12 is survey only, with no curves and 0 checkshot levels, so there is nothing to grade over the zone.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 10, 'Suppose the pack''s PHIT, VSH and SW for Ekene-1 were read into the usability matrix as its porosity, shale volume and saturation curves. How would its cell then read?', '["Good, because every other item at Ekene-1 already grades good and a cell takes the worst of its items.", "Still limited, because the pack curves are the kit''s interpretation and the matrix grades an interpretation below a log.", "Missing, since curves brought in from outside the LAS files are read by the matrix as absent over the zone.", "Limited until the issue register''s 31 lines are each kept, edited or dismissed by the user."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ekene-1 grades good on sonic, density, shear, checkshots, datum and survey, and limited only on porosity, vsh and sw. The 25 checkshot levels are well above the two the rule needs.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 11, 'A new well arrives with sonic, density, porosity, shale volume, saturation, checkshots, a set elevation and a survey, but no shear sonic. What would the issue register suggest for it?', '["One high issue, for shear, because every shear-dependent result at the well would then rest on an estimate.", "No issue at all, since Greenberg-Castagna supplies shear from Vp and the cell grades good with the estimate in place.", "One medium issue, for shear: an absent shear is limited, and a limited item is a medium issue.", "Two medium issues, one for shear and one for the Vp/Vs that a missing shear sonic leaves without a measurement."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Shear, porosity, shale volume and saturation are limited when absent, because the study can go on with an estimate. A missing item is a high issue and a limited one medium, one issue per well and item kind.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 12, 'A well has every curve but only one checkshot level and an unset elevation. How does its usability cell read?', '["Limited, because both shortfalls can be worked around with an estimate and neither stops the rock physics from running.", "Missing, because an unset elevation is missing and the cell takes the worst of its items.", "Good, since sonic and density are present and those are the only two items the matrix treats as required.", "Limited for the checkshots, with the elevation ignored because the matrix reads datum only for a deviated well."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Fewer than two checkshot levels is limited, and an unset elevation is missing. One missing item makes the cell missing however good the rest.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 13, 'Could the course''s oil-to-brine substitution run on the logs of Ekene-5 to Ekene-10 as the headers stand?', '["Yes, at every one of them, because the substitution reads only Vp, Vs and density, and those come from DT, DTS and RHOB.", "Yes, but only at the deviated wells, since a deviation survey is what lets the engine place each sample in the oil leg.", "Not until porosity, shale volume and saturation curves are brought in: substitution reads each sample''s PHIT, Vsh and Sw, and the pack carries those for Ekene-1 to Ekene-4 alone.", "No, because the usability cells of those wells are missing."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The method mixes minerals on the sample''s Vsh, mixes the in situ fluid by Wood at its Sw, and runs Gassmann with its PHIT. Those three curves read as absent in every LAS file, so the cells are limited, and the pack adds them for four wells only.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 14, 'Why do the measured depths of Ekene-1 to Ekene-4 read straight down, when Ekene-8 to Ekene-10 need their surveys?', '["The four pack wells are vertical; Ekene-8, Ekene-9 and Ekene-10 are deviated.", "The four pack wells have no deviation survey at all, so the matrix grades their survey item limited and assumes vertical.", "Measured depth below the kelly bushing is defined as vertical depth for every well, so no survey ever changes it.", "The kit writes the pack wells in feet and the deviated wells in metres, and a metre depth needs a survey to convert."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Depths are measured depth below the kelly bushing in feet, and the pack wells are vertical. The inventory lists Ekene-8 to Ekene-10 as deviated, and their survey items grade good.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 15, 'The Ekene full stack is measured in a shallow window and a deep one. Which figures does the feasibility verdict quote for the band and the signal?', '["The 1600 to 2400 ms figures, a peak of 21.761176 Hz and a median of 77.008444, because the deepest window is the hardest test.", "The 800 to 2400 ms figures, a -6 dB band of 15.790410 to 34.601268 Hz, because the longest window gives the steadiest spectrum.", "The single Ekene-1 trace, -6 dB 30.556053 to 32.127840 Hz, because the verdict is written for the well where the sand is logged.", "The 800 to 1600 ms figures, the window that holds the Ekene Sand: -6 dB 14.425434 to 34.787627 Hz and a median of 361.155122."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The spectrum window 800 to 1600 ms holds the Ekene Sand, and the verdict''s band and signal rows read that window: 14.425434 to 34.787627 Hz, peak 31.374887 Hz, and 361.155122 (25.576938 dB).'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 16, 'A draft verdict says the band at the target is 30.556053 to 32.127840 Hz at -6 dB. What did its writer measure?', '["The mean spectrum of the 120 spread traces with no taper, which narrows the band by leaving the trace ends sharp.", "The spectrum of the single trace at Ekene-1, whose -6 dB band is a sliver around its peak.", "The -20 dB edges of the mean spectrum, read at 10^(-20/20) of the peak amplitude in place of 10^(-6/20).", "The deep window of 1600 to 2400 ms, where absorption has narrowed the band of the full stack."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That trace''s peak of 31.418177 Hz and centroid of 26.664566 Hz land near the mean''s 31.374887 and 26.656573 Hz; only its band collapses. The verdict''s band row needs the mean of stated traces.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 17, 'The Ekene mean spectrum''s peak amplitude is 3269.099145, above every tabled amplitude, the largest of which is 2691.535156 at 24.902344 Hz. Why?', '["The peak is refined between frequency steps by a parabola through the three highest values.", "The peak is read from a single trace at Ekene-1, whose spiky spectrum stands above the mean of the 120 spread traces.", "The table is printed after the taper of 0.100000 and the peak before it, so the two amplitudes come from different traces.", "averageSpectrum sums the amplitude of the traces at the peak and averages it elsewhere, which lifts the peak alone."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The frequency step is 0.488281 Hz and the peak falls between steps at 31.374887 Hz; the parabola through the highest three values places it there and can sit above any tabled amplitude.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 18, 'The coherency method turns the correlation c of two neighbouring traces into a power ratio c / (1 - c). What does it assume about the two traces?', '["They carry independent signal and the same noise, so their correlation measures how much noise the pair shares.", "They are separated by exactly one bin, with no dip between them, so the lag search is never needed.", "They carry the same signal and independent noise of equal power.", "Both are tapered at 0.100000 first."]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is Hatton, Worthington and Makin''s form. On the 20 pairs along inline 1027, 800 to 1600 ms, the median is 361.155122, which is 25.576938 dB as 10 log10 of a power ratio.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 19, 'A neighbouring pair on some other survey returns a signal to noise of 999998.999971. How should a study read it?', '["As an exceptionally clean dataset, far cleaner than the Ekene shallow window''s median of 361.155122 and ready for any attribute.", "Only as nearly identical traces: the correlation has hit the engine''s cap and this is that cap''s ratio.", "As a refusal, since the engine''s own words for neighbouring traces of nulls are \"No neighbouring pair had samples to correlate.\"", "As an amplitude ratio, to be put in decibels with 20 log10."]'::jsonb, 1, 'With c / (1 - c), a correlation near 1 sends the power ratio up without bound; capped at 0.999999 it stops at 999998.999971. QI Studio prints any ratio of 10,000 or more as "over 10,000 (over 40 dB)" for that reason.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 20, 'The Ekene crossline stripe reports 8.500000 cycles at a period of 15.058824 lines. How is that cycles figure obtained, and does it satisfy its part of the rule?', '["Repeats counted along the inline axis, 128 over 13.473684; it falls short of the ten cycles the rule asks for.", "The prominence over the share, 27.724874 over 0.015148, which the rule needs to be at least five before a stripe is called.", "The period divided by the shortest period searched, 15.058824 over 2, which clears the rule''s minimum of three.", "Lines in the slice over the period, 128 over 15.058824; it clears the five repeats the rule asks for."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Cycles count how often the period repeats across the 128 crosslines of the slice. That test and the prominence test are met here; it is the share, 0.015148 against the 0.3 needed, that stops the call.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 21, 'The designed teaching stripe multiplies every sixth crossline by 1.2. What does the search along the inlines return on that slice?', '["A period of 13.473684 lines, share 0.014803 and no footprint: the axis the stripe was not added to does not move.", "A period of 5.953488 lines with share 0.490123, because a gain stripe on one axis shows equally on the other.", "A refusal, since a slice with a stripe added by hand falls short of the engine''s minimum of 4 by 4.", "A period of six lines exactly, share above 0.3, called a footprint on both axes by the rule."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Along the crosslines the designed stripe is found at 5.953488 lines with share 0.490123 and prominence 467.977933. Along the inlines the slice still reads 13.473684 lines and a share of a few hundredths.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 22, 'A well in the Ekene Sand water leg has no shear log and is filled with Greenberg-Castagna. Judged on Ekene-1, which way is that estimate biased?', '["High: a Vp/Vs on it would read too low.", "Not at all: the coefficients fit rock like this.", "Low: the regression gives less shear than this rock carries.", "Low in the shale and high in the sand, because gcSandShaleVs mixes the two on Vsh and the sand term overshoots."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Over the 103 water-leg samples measured over predicted averages 1.036576, and every tabled row is above 1, from 1.020609 to 1.062182.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 23, 'Over the oil-leg samples measured / Greenberg-Castagna reads 1.151143, and substitution to brine moves Vs only a little. What one fact about the rock ties the two together?', '["Oil stiffens the shear modulus of the frame, so measured Vs rises in the oil leg and substitution to brine raises it again.", "The pore fluid leaves the shear modulus alone: the oil softens Vp without touching shear, and substitution keeps the shear modulus fixed.", "The oil leg holds more samples than the water leg, so its mean ratio is less affected by the noise of single samples.", "Greenberg-Castagna predicts shear from density alone."]'::jsonb, 1, 'In the oil leg the regression reads a softened Vp and predicts too little shear. Substitution keeps the shear modulus and swaps the density, so Vs at 5082.000000 ft moves only from 1358.537000 to 1349.137957 m/s.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 24, 'At Ekene-1 the cap rock reads Vp/Vs 2.916045 and Poisson''s ratio 0.433363. How do the two legs compare?', '["Both legs sit above the cap rock, since a sand that carries hydrocarbon raises its Vp/Vs and its Poisson''s ratio together.", "The oil leg sits above the cap rock and the water leg below it, because the oil lowers Vs further than it lowers Vp.", "All three layers read close together, since each mean is taken with the same Vsh cut-off of 0.350000.", "Both legs sit well below it: the oil leg at 1.749257 and 0.257270, the water leg at 1.827552 and 0.286320."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The elastic set on the layer means puts the Ogbia Shale cap rock far above either leg in Vp/Vs and Poisson''s ratio. The cap rock is a stated 65 ft of log, with no cut-off applied to it.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 25, 'A study pools Ekene-1 to Ekene-4 to calibrate its Vs from Vp relation on their brine-bearing clean sand. How does the pooled fit compare with Ekene-1 alone?', '["465 samples give slope 872.045751 and s 39.612304 m/s, against 855.280029 and 41.009437 m/s at Ekene-1.", "It returns the same slope of 855.280029, since every pack well sits on the same Ekene earth model and the fit is linear.", "It refuses, because the four wells'' water legs span different depths and the engine fits one well at a time.", "The slope settles onto the Greenberg-Castagna sandstone a1 of 0.804160."]'::jsonb, 0, 'More wells move the slope. At 3100 m/s the pooled fit predicts 1681.273188 m/s against 1680.985632 at Ekene-1.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 26, 'The capstone grades the slope of the local shear trend. In what unit is it read, and on which samples is it fitted?', '["The slope in m/s per m/s, a dimensionless ratio, fitted on every net sand sample of the case well, both legs together.", "The ratio of measured over Greenberg-Castagna Vs, fitted on the case''s oil leg where the fluid effect is largest.", "c1 in m/s of Vs per km/s of Vp, fitted on the case''s water-leg net sand in the form the case states.", "c0 in m/s, fitted on the cap rock, because the intercept is the term the shale fixes."]'::jsonb, 2, 'fitVsRegression regresses Vs in m/s on Vp in km/s, so the slope c1 is in m/s per km/s; at Ekene-1 it is 855.280029. The capstone fits the case''s water-leg net sand.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 27, 'Which comparison at Ekene-1 isolates what the oil alone does to AI (acoustic impedance)?', '["The oil leg against the measured water leg, 6639018.603084 against 7358819.677147 kg/(m2 s), since both legs sit in one sand.", "The oil leg against the same samples taken to brine: the mean AI rises 7.456776 percent, 6641244.924742 to 7136467.682134 kg/(m2 s).", "The cap rock against the oil leg, 4697628.723902 against 6639018.603084 kg/(m2 s), the contrast at the top of the sand.", "The live oil against the brine, 0.852881 against 2.662550 GPa."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Substitution keeps each sample''s porosity and clay and swaps only the pore fluid. The legs are different rock, oil leg porosity 0.203626 and Vsh 0.178134 against 0.197677 and 0.117854.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 28, 'Calibrated at Ekene-1, fitVsRegression reports 101 degrees of freedom beside its coefficients. How is that figure reached?', '["103 less the two samples at the ends of the Vp range, 2670.381000 and 3481.638000 m/s, which fix the calibration limits.", "The 103 samples less the two that sit nearest the oil-water contact, which the fit sets aside as mixed fluid.", "The samples left after the two refusal checks remove any pair with no measured Vs, so it depends on the log.", "n less the number of coefficients: 103 samples less the two of a linear fit, c0 and c1."]'::jsonb, 3, 'fitVsRegression returns n, the degrees of freedom, the residual standard error s of 41.009437 m/s and R squared 0.925483. A quadratic fit adds c2, a third coefficient.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 29, 'At the oil-leg mean Sw of 0.574254, the Wood mix gives a pore-fluid modulus of 1.398867 GPa. Where does that sit between the two phases?', '["Midway, because Wood''s average is the volume-weighted mean of the two moduli and the saturation is close to one half.", "Near the brine, since water is the wetting phase and a mix of more than half brine behaves as brine.", "Near the softer phase: live oil is 0.852881 GPa and brine 2.662550 GPa, and the Wood average stays near the softer one until the brine fraction is high.", "Above both phases, because mixing two fluids stiffens the mixture past either one alone."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Wood''s mix is the uniform (Reuss) mix. At Sw 0.500000 it reads 1.291926 GPa and at 0.800000 1.869288 GPa, both still well below the brine''s 2.662550 GPa.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 30, 'At 180 degF and 3200 psia, how do brine and live oil compare in bulk modulus, the property substitution turns on?', '["The oil is the stiffer of the two, since an oil of 32 API carrying gas at 400 scf/stb is denser than brine at a salinity of 0.035000.", "Brine reads 2.662550 GPa and the live oil 0.852881 GPa.", "They read the same once both are converted to SI, because Batzle-Wang returns one modulus for every pore fluid at a given pressure.", "Brine reads 1.398867 GPa and the oil 0.987056 GPa, the two moduli the Wood mix returns at reservoir conditions."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Batzle and Wang at the stated conditions give brine 1005.203569 kg/m3 and 1627.503313 m/s, live oil 754.214377 kg/m3 and 1063.400328 m/s: the brine is denser and far stiffer.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 31, 'The oil-leg sample at 5114.000000 ft (PHIT 0.184200, Vsh 0.072400, Sw 0.786200) has an in situ Vp of 3129.781000 m/s. What Vp does substitution to brine return there?', '["3129.781000 m/s, unchanged, as the sample is mostly brine.", "3088.285476 m/s, the brine Vp at 5110.000000 ft.", "1788.275833 m/s, read as Vp from the Vs column.", "3248.090773 m/s, from the sample''s own PHIT, Vsh and Sw."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every sample with oil in it goes through the substitution with its own PHIT, Vsh and Sw: at 5114.000000 ft the in situ fluid modulus is 1.831634 GPa, Vp goes to 3248.090773 m/s, Vs to 1788.275833 m/s and density from 2348.600000 to 2358.484446 kg/m3.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 32, 'Substitution to brine raises the density of the 5082.000000 ft sample from 2311.300000 to 2343.616460 kg/m3. What sets the size of that rise?', '["The porosity times the gap between the brine density and the in situ fluid density.", "The rise in the dry-rock modulus, since Gassmann stiffens the frame when brine enters the pores and packs the grains.", "The mineral modulus of 30.444339 GPa, raised by the brine.", "The shear modulus, which grows with the fluid density."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The shear modulus does not change, and the density swaps the pore fluid: rho_new = rho + phi (rho_brine - rho_fluid). Brine is 1005.203569 kg/m3 and live oil 754.214377 at the stated conditions.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 33, 'One interpreter reads the Ekene-1 brine top''s distance from the trend fitted to the well''s substituted boundaries; another reads it from Castagna, Swan and Foster. They get -0.004519 and -0.363599. Who is wrong?', '["The second: the published slope needs a Gardner exponent the logs lack.", "The first, since a line fitted on one well''s boundaries cannot be checked and the published line is the reference.", "Neither: the two lines differ, and which one a distance is read from is a stated choice.", "Both, since a brine top must read zero from any wet trend."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The fitted slope at 16 ft blocks is -2.257963 and the published one at Vs/Vp 0.408987 is -0.404345. A lesson names which line it means, and a verdict states it.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 34, 'Three block lengths, 8, 16 and 24 ft, are tried on the Ekene-1 wet trend. Which statement about its interfaces and slopes holds?', '["Interfaces stay at 24 and only the slope moves, because blocking changes the means and leaves the boundaries where they are.", "Interfaces fall from 49 to 24 to 16, and the slope moves from -2.565814 to -2.257963 to -2.254272.", "Interfaces rise with the block length, since a longer block holds more samples and so more boundaries between them.", "Nothing changes: block length is a display setting and the line is fitted on the raw samples."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Blocks carry their mean Vp, Vs and density, and each boundary between two blocks gives one (A, B) point. The rms distance reads 0.006424, 0.006806 and 0.005925 across the three.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 35, 'Before the Ekene-1 wet trend is fitted over the whole pack window, which samples are taken to brine?', '["Only the 65 oil-leg net sand samples, since the cap rock and the shale above carry no saturation curve to substitute.", "All 800 samples, because a wet trend is defined on brine and every sample has its fluid replaced whatever its Sw.", "The 103 water-leg samples alone.", "Every sample with oil in it, Sw below 1: 79 of the 800."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The logs are then blocked at 16 ft, 32 samples a block, giving 25 blocks and 24 interfaces, and each interface gives an intercept and a gradient by Shuey.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 36, 'At the top of the Ekene Sand, which measure separates the oil from the brine case and which does not?', '["The distance from the fitted wet trend does, -0.072091 against -0.004519; the class does not, class I both ways.", "The class does, class III in situ against class I with brine; the distance does not, since both points sit on the trend.", "Neither does: the intercepts of 0.170742 and 0.205564 lie inside the 0.02 band, so both cases read the same.", "Both do, since the class changes from I to II exactly where the distance crosses zero."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Against the fitted line the in situ interface lies 0.067572 further to the hydrocarbon side than the same rock with brine. Both intercepts are well above 0.02, so both are class I.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 37, 'On the Ekene-1 wet trend, the boundary whose lower block sits at 5059.500000 ft gives A 0.000627 and B -0.001871. How would avoClass name it?', '["Class III, because B is at or below zero and that is the condition for class III whatever the intercept.", "Class II, because A lies within 0.02 of zero.", "Class I, since its intercept is positive.", "Class IV, as a small positive A with a falling gradient is the class IV pattern of Rutherford and Williams."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The band is 0.02 either side of zero and 0.000627 lies inside it, so the sign of B does not enter. The Ekene top''s A of 0.170742 sits above the band and reads class I.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 38, 'The modelled Ekene-1 top is dimmer with oil at every angle. How does the gap between the in situ and brine coefficients change from near to far?', '["It narrows, from 0.066214 near the normal to 0.034809 at 30 degrees, so the near stack separates the two cases best.", "It stays at 0.034809 at every angle, because both cases share one cap rock above the interface and its contrast is fixed.", "It reverses, the in situ coefficient rising above the brine one beyond 25 degrees as the oil curve turns back up.", "It widens: 0.034809 at normal incidence and 0.066214 at 30 degrees, and the far means read 0.073416 against 0.144237."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The near means read 0.156172 against 0.192995 and the far 0.073416 against 0.144237: the in situ coefficient is below the brine one throughout, and further below at the far angles.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 39, 'With brine in the oil leg, shuey returns A 0.205564 and B -0.475316 for the modelled Ekene-1 top. What curvature C does it return beside them?', '["0.186597, as the fluid leaves C alone.", "0.206058, the exact coefficient at 0 degrees.", "0.216617, beside the in situ C of 0.186597.", "0.166006, the exact coefficient at 35 degrees."]'::jsonb, 2, 'shuey returns the intercept, the gradient and the curvature of the interface: A 0.205564, B -0.475316 and C 0.216617 with brine, against A 0.170742, B -0.563558 and C 0.186597 in situ. 0.206058 and 0.166006 are exact coefficients from zoeppritzRpp at 0 and 35 degrees.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 40, 'Which of these belongs to the interpreter and to no engine in a feasibility study of the Ekene Sand?', '["Writing the verdict on whether the study should go ahead, with the choices behind each number stated beside it.", "Computing the -6 dB edges of the mean spectrum of the stated traces.", "Naming the class of the top interface from its intercept and gradient, which avoClass returns.", "Fitting the fluid line through the origin to the brine-substituted interfaces by total least squares, which fitFluidLine returns."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Each of the other three is a function''s output: spectrumStats gives the edges, avoClass the class and fitFluidLine the line. Which samples are net sand or legs is also picked before any function runs.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 41, 'Between 30 and 35 degrees, what do the exact coefficients of the modelled Ekene-1 top do with oil and with brine?', '["Both turn up together, since the critical angle of the cap rock is reached at 30 degrees in either case.", "The brine coefficient turns up, 0.137212 to 0.166006, while the in situ one keeps falling, 0.070998 to 0.060228.", "Both keep falling at the same rate, so the gap between them stays at 0.066214 out to 35 degrees.", "The in situ coefficient turns up and crosses above the brine one, as oil brightens the top at the far angles."]'::jsonb, 1, 'From 25 to 30 degrees both still fall, 0.094393 to 0.070998 in situ and 0.145543 to 0.137212 with brine; only the brine curve turns by 35 degrees.'),
  ('qi', 'beginner', 'final', null, 42, 'Which of these is one of the six figures the Associate capstone grades?', '["The median signal to noise of the case traces.", "The class of the case''s top interface.", "The usability grade of the case well, read from the matrix after the case file has been pasted into the panel.", "The AI and the Vp/Vs of the stated sample after substitution to brine."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The six are the -6 dB low edge and the centroid of the case traces'' mean spectrum, the slope of the local shear trend, the substituted AI and Vp/Vs of the stated sample, and the distance from the case''s own fitted wet trend.');
